axis: structural-topology
axis_gloss: "whether a thing hangs from exactly one parent, or may belong to several containers at once"
poles:
  a: multi-parent-graph
  b: single-parent-tree
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: axis-only
leverage: 10.5
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(7) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.5)"

question: >
  On structural topology — whether a thing hangs from exactly one parent, or may
  belong to several containers at once — the records assert both multi parent
  graph (3 judgments, 3 authored) and single parent tree (4 judgments, 1
  authored). Which should be the stated rule, and where is the exception?

why_it_matters: >
  Until this is answered, a record below Dave inherits whichever side it happens
  to copy. Full draft pending: the proposal batch for this axis did not
  complete; the axis, poles, counts and evidence are real, the options below are
  the generic form.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    3 judgments sit at multi-parent-graph (3 authored) and 4 at single-parent-tree
    (1 authored). The repository asserts both directions on this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    The full proposal.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      multi parent graph becomes the stated rule across its scope.
    cost: "not yet drafted"
    risk: "not yet drafted"
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "not yet drafted"
    evidence_for:
      - "J0155"
      - "J0162"
      - "J0803"
  - name: B
    description: >
      single parent tree becomes the stated rule across its scope.
    cost: "not yet drafted"
    risk: "not yet drafted"
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "not yet drafted"
    evidence_for:
      - "J0172"
      - "J0221"
      - "J0411"
      - "J1215"
  - name: C
    description: >
      One pole is the rule; the other is a written carve-out for a named case.
    cost: "not yet drafted"
    risk: "not yet drafted"
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "not yet drafted"
    evidence_for:
      []

recommendation:
  option_ref: 
  confidence: 0
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    Not drafted. Re-run the decision-proposal workflow for this axis; the
    recommendation must be argued from the evidence, not defaulted.

